# ── Removal-failure classification (T-10891) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A sweep that removes NOTHING used to exit 0 exactly like a clean one, so under the 30-minute host
# crontab (wrapped by `cron-notify.sh`, which alerts off the EXIT CODE) a totally useless run was
# indistinguishable from a healthy one, indefinitely. The fix is NOT "exit non-zero on any removal
# failure": the residue that triggers it TODAY is permanent and unfixable by the sweeping user
# (foreign-owned dirs under the /tmp sticky bit), so that would page every 30 minutes about a
# condition nobody can fix — the alert gets silenced, and a silenced alert reads as coverage. Both
# that option and "fail only when ZERO removals succeeded" were adjudicated and KILLED by the
# external auditor (`decisions/sweep-failure-loudness-audit-adhoc.yaml`, YELLOW). What survives is
# classification AT THE POINT OF FAILURE — with the auditor's own counter-argument adopted as the
# bound: classification is the first step toward bespoke alert tuning, so the classifier stays
# MECHANICAL, LOCAL and TINY — ONE named POSIX impossibility class, no growing taxonomy, no retry
# policy, no owner-specific exceptions. A SECOND class is not an implementation detail to add
# quietly; it is the signal to STOP and escalate.
#
# THE SECOND CLASS — ADDED THROUGH THAT GATE, NOT AROUND IT (T-10976). There are now TWO recognised
# classes, and the bound above still holds against a THIRD:
#   (1) PERMANENT — foreign-owned under the /tmp sticky bit (`_removal_failure_is_expected_residue`).
#       Unfixable by this user forever, hence recorded ONCE and never alerted on.
#   (2) TRANSIENT — the ACL-protected `.yitc/transcript-archive` leftover that `land` LEAVES BY DESIGN
#       (`_acl_protected_archive_leftover`; the producer is `_recover_unremovable_worktree_leftover`).
#       This one is not a new excuse the sweep invented for itself: it is a SIBLING VERB'S deliberate
#       output, so the pre-change exit 1 was two verbs disagreeing about one residue, not a defect
#       report. It escalated to the owner first (the */30 cron alerted on EIGHT such dirs, 2026-08-12,
#       hand-cleared twice) and was root-caused to the producer before this card was filed. It gets NO
#       journal record, unlike (1): it CLEARS once the acl-watcher unlocks, and a once-only record of a
#       condition that ends would be a permanent line about a temporary fact.
# A THIRD class re-arms the STOP: stop and escalate, do not add it quietly here.
_RESIDUE_FINGERPRINT = "worktree-sweep-permission-impossible-residue"
_RESIDUE_SCAN_BYTES = 4 << 20   # T-10897: the once-guard's BOUNDED tail (4 MiB ≈ the last few days of
                                # appends). A record that scrolls past it costs ONE re-record, never a
                                # per-run stream; an unbounded read costs 0.40s on every 30-min run.


