# ── SPEC-0184 rule 3 — merge-and-evict, BEFORE the pass is spent (T-11193) ────────────────────────
# The removal reasons this step can produce. EXACTLY ONE of them is journal-bearing — `conflict` —
# and the split is the substance of the rule, not bookkeeping:
#
#   * `conflict` is a fact about the CHANGES, discoverable ONLY here. Nothing else in the system would
#     ever learn that A and B are textually incompatible, because pre-change they never share a tree
#     until one of them has already reached `main`. So it earns the durable `evicted-for-conflict` row.
#   * `unevaluable` / `head-unmergeable` are facts about THIS ATTEMPT — a branch that could not be
#     read, a git error, a slot holder that does not merge onto `main` at all. Journaling them as a
#     member verdict would invite a reader to treat a transient failure as a property of the change.
#
# NEITHER kind needs a requeue action, and that is the point rather than an omission (audit-pre
# finding, absorbed mode-b 2026-08-16). A member is a branch name read off ANOTHER land process's own
# `waiting_for_*` heartbeat; that process is blocked inside its own land, holding its own place in the
# queue. This step never signals it, never touches its branch, and writes no batch state it could
# read — so removing it from THIS candidate leaves it exactly where the pre-change path left it, and
# it takes its own slot and verifies alone. Requeueing is not a step that could be skipped; it is what
# happens when nothing is done. T-11192's formation REMAINDER already ships this identical property.
_LAND_MEMBER_REMOVAL_EMITS_VERDICT = ("conflict",)


