def _registered_worktree_paths(main_wt: Path, *, _run_git_cap) -> "set[str]":
    """The paths git ITSELF registers as worktrees of `main_wt`, resolved.

    Parsed EXACTLY off the `worktree <path>` records — a substring test would false-match a sibling
    worktree whose path merely has another's as a prefix (`.../wt` vs `.../wt-2`). Resolved on both
    sides so a symlinked checkout path still matches its registration. An unreadable/failing
    `worktree list` yields the EMPTY set, which is the fail-closed direction for every caller here:
    "git does not vouch for this path" ⇒ do nothing.
    """
    out = set()
    try:
        listing = _run_git_cap(["worktree", "list", "--porcelain"], main_wt).stdout
    except Exception:                          # noqa: BLE001 — unreadable registry ⇒ vouch for nothing
        return out
    for ln in listing.splitlines():
        if not ln.startswith("worktree "):
            continue
        try:
            out.add(str(Path(ln[len("worktree "):]).resolve()))
        except OSError:
            continue
    return out


def _recover_unremovable_worktree_leftover(W: Path, main_wt: Path, *, _run_git_cap) -> "str | None":
    """T-10370 + T-11934: `git worktree remove --force` can still fail, leaving the worktree W
    half-removed — git's `.git` file gone but its REGISTRATION intact — so the ff-merged branch is
    undeletable and a bare `fatal: ... is not a working tree` reaches the caller together with a
    `remove manually: git worktree remove --force W` cue that RE-FAILS on the same fatal.

    Complete the deregistration ourselves: best-effort remove everything removable, `git worktree
    prune` the now-broken registration, and REPORT what survived. On success return an explicit
    actionable operator message (the caller prints it, deletes the ff-merged branch, and continues
    the normal success tail). Return None only when deregistration did NOT complete — a genuine
    fs/lock failure — so the caller falls back to the bare-fatal warn unchanged.

    T-11934 — RECOGNITION IS ON THE STATE, NOT ON A CAUSE-SPECIFIC MARKER. The T-10370 shape of this
    helper returned None unless `W/.yitc/transcript-archive` existed, so it recognised exactly ONE
    producer of undeletable residue and every other one fell straight through to the bare fatal.
    MEASURED (aiseller, 2026-08-31, X-1203): a verify CONTAINER left root-owned `__pycache__`
    directories in the worktree; the removal failed with `not a working tree`, the printed remedy
    re-failed, and the operator hand-verified and hand-cleaned. Both reported causes are ONE
    behaviour — residue this user cannot delete makes git stop mid-removal, and the recovery was
    gated on WHICH residue rather than on the fact of it. So the archive check is no longer an
    admission gate; it only chooses WHICH message names the survivors.

    WHAT IT STILL WILL NOT DO — the card's own directive, «deleting harder as a reflex is the option
    that adds privilege»: nothing here chmods, chowns, or elevates. An undeletable survivor is NAMED
    and LEFT, never forced. The honest upstream fix for a container-produced root-owned artefact is
    the container's user mapping, which lives in the PRODUCING project's repo, not in this kernel
    path (§Scope-boundary discipline) — it is routed there, and this path's job is to stop failing
    opaquely, not to acquire the privilege to delete what it should not have to.

    AC3 GUARD — the delete target is DERIVED, never asserted. `shutil.rmtree` below is unconditional
    on whatever `W` is handed in, and the enclosing step also runs a `branch -D`: together that is a
    shape which, pointed at the wrong path, discards real work. So before touching anything, admit
    only a path that is PROVABLY a torn-down or registered worktree of THIS repo:

      (1) `W` must not overlap the MAIN checkout — not equal to it, not an ancestor of it, not
          inside it. That is the input which would destroy the repository itself.
      (2) `W` must be EITHER still registered as a worktree of `main_wt`, OR no longer a git
          checkout at all (`git -C W rev-parse --git-dir` fails). Both are states this recovery
          exists to finish; a LIVE git checkout that is not this repo's worktree is somebody
          else's tree and is refused.

    BOTH STATES ARE REAL, WHICH IS WHY (2) IS A DISJUNCTION — measured, not assumed. `git worktree
    remove --force` blocked by undeletable residue DEREGISTERS the worktree and deletes its `.git`
    link FIRST, then fails on the residue: the path arrives here already unregistered, and the
    remedy the pre-fix code printed (`git worktree remove --force W`) then answers `fatal: ... is
    not a working tree` — which is exactly the sequence X-1203 reported. A failure that stops
    EARLIER (a lock) leaves the registration intact, which is the T-10370 state `worktree prune`
    below is for. Requiring registration alone would have refused the very case this card is about.

    THE HONEST RESIDUAL, stated rather than papered over: an arbitrary directory that is not a git
    checkout and does not overlap the main checkout is indistinguishable BY INSPECTION from a
    torn-down worktree, so the guard cannot refuse it. What it does refuse is every input carrying
    live git-tracked work, plus the repository itself. The caller supplies only the frozen `W`
    captured at `cmd_land` step 1 — the recorded worktree path — so no reachable path today
    supplies anything else; the guard is the belt against a future caller, not the sole proof.
    """
    import shutil
    try:
        W_res, main_res = W.resolve(), main_wt.resolve()
    except OSError:
        return None
    if W_res == main_res or W_res in main_res.parents or main_res in W_res.parents:
        return None                            # (1) never the main checkout, its parent, or inside it
    if str(W_res) not in _registered_worktree_paths(main_wt, _run_git_cap=_run_git_cap):
        # (2) not registered — admit ONLY if it is no longer a git checkout at all.
        try:
            probe = _run_git_cap(["rev-parse", "--git-dir"], W)
        except Exception:                      # noqa: BLE001 — cannot probe ⇒ cannot vouch
            return None
        if getattr(probe, "returncode", 1) == 0:
            return None                        # a LIVE checkout that is not our worktree — refuse
    archive = W / ".yitc" / "transcript-archive"
    had_archive = archive.exists()
    shutil.rmtree(W, ignore_errors=True)       # remove all we can; leaves what we cannot unlink
    _run_git_cap(["worktree", "prune"], main_wt)       # deregister the now-broken (missing-.git) worktree
    if str(W_res) in _registered_worktree_paths(main_wt, _run_git_cap=_run_git_cap):
        return None  # could not deregister — surface the genuine failure via the caller
    if not W.exists():
        # Everything came away — nothing left behind, whatever the residue had been.
        return ("residue blocked the git-side removal but was removable — worktree fully cleaned"
                if not had_archive else
                "an ACL-protected .yitc/transcript-archive was present but removable — worktree fully cleaned")
    survivors = list(W.rglob("*"))
    # Is every survivor the ACL archive (or an ancestor dir that exists only to hold it)? That is the
    # T-10370 case and keeps its own wording, which downstream readers (the `worktree sweep`
    # classifier, the operator runbooks) already recognise.
    archive_ancestors = {W / ".yitc", archive}
    acl_only = had_archive and not any(
        not (p == archive or archive in p.parents or p in archive_ancestors) for p in survivors)
    if acl_only:
        return (f"an ACL-protected .yitc/transcript-archive subtree could not be deleted and was LEFT IN "
                f"PLACE — chmod-closed by the acl-watcher (the frozen memory/transcript archive; 0 repo "
                f"files, cosmetic disk leftover): {archive}\n"
                f"      remove manually once the acl-watcher unlocks it: rm -rf {W}")
    # Any other residue: NAME it. Report the top-most undeletable paths (a root-owned `__pycache__`
    # tree yields hundreds of children and one useful parent), never a bare "cleanup failed".
    _sset = set(survivors)
    tops = sorted({str(p) for p in survivors if not any(q in p.parents for q in _sset)})
    shown = tops[:10]
    more = f" (+{len(tops) - len(shown)} top-most more)" if len(tops) > len(shown) else ""
    # The COUNT is every survivor; the LIST is the top-most ones. A root-owned `__pycache__` tree
    # survives as a parent chain plus its leaves, and printing all of them buries the one path the
    # operator needs to look at — but printing only the tops while claiming "1 path" would understate
    # what is on disk. So: honest count, actionable list.
    return (f"{len(survivors)} path(s) could NOT be deleted by this user and were LEFT IN PLACE — the "
            f"worktree is deregistered and the landed work is safe on main, but this directory is "
            f"NOT cleaned up; top-most: {', '.join(shown)}{more}\n"
            f"      nothing was force-deleted and no privilege was escalated. If these are "
            f"root-owned build artefacts from a verify container, the durable fix is that "
            f"container's user mapping in the project that runs it; to clear this one now: "
            f"sudo rm -rf {W}")


_DETACHED_MARKER_NAME = "WORKTREE-DETACHED.md"


def _mark_detached_worktree_leftover(W: Path, branch: str, landed_sha: str) -> "str | None":
    """T-11074 (X-0861): make a post-land SURVIVING worktree directory SELF-IDENTIFYING.

    A successful land removes the worktree REGISTRATION but does not always remove the DIRECTORY —
    the ACL-archive recovery path above deregisters and deliberately leaves the dir on disk, and any
    future deregister-without-delete path lands here too. The directory then LOOKS alive: same path,
    same files, same editor buffers — but it is no longer a git checkout, so every further edit is
    outside git and every `work commit` fails with a bare "not inside a git checkout". MEASURED
    (kupiclub X-0861, 2026-08-13): during an emergency production fix the author kept editing there
    for several minutes, and recovery meant hand-copying files into a fresh worktree.

    So on the success tail: if `W` survives, write a marker INTO it naming the landed branch + sha,
    and return ONE stdout line naming the directory. Announcing beats deleting — the directory may
    hold untracked files the author still wants, and silently deleting an author's work is the worse
    failure (the card's own directive).

    Returns None when `W` is gone — the normal fully-removed land writes no marker and prints no
    stale warning (the complement AC). NEVER raises: the land already succeeded at the ff, so a
    cleanup-stage failure must stay benign (the step-7 contract) — an unwritable marker degrades to
    the announcement line alone, which is the half that reaches the author who is watching stdout.
    """
    try:
        if not W.exists():
            return None
    except OSError:
        return None
    marker = W / _DETACHED_MARKER_NAME
    body = (
        f"# This directory is NO LONGER a git worktree\n"
        f"\n"
        f"`{branch}` was landed to main (now `{landed_sha}`) and its git worktree registration was\n"
        f"removed, but this DIRECTORY could not be deleted, so it was left in place.\n"
        f"\n"
        f"- Nothing you edit here is tracked by git any more — `git` and `bin/yitc-v2 work commit`\n"
        f"  will fail here with \"not inside a git checkout\".\n"
        f"- The landed work is safe: it is on `main` at `{landed_sha}`.\n"
        f"- To keep working: open a fresh worktree (`bin/yitc-v2 worktree new --work <slug>`), copy\n"
        f"  anything you still want out of this directory, then `rm -rf {W}`.\n"
        f"\n"
        f"Written by `land` (T-11074, after incident X-0861).\n"
    )
    wrote = True
    try:
        marker.write_text(body, encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        wrote = False
    note = (f"      it left a marker: {marker}" if wrote
            else f"      (could not write the {_DETACHED_MARKER_NAME} marker here — this line is the only notice)")
    return (f"land: NOTE — this directory is DETACHED: it is no longer a git worktree, but it still "
            f"exists on disk: {W}\n"
            f"      further edits there are outside git and are NOT part of {branch} (landed at "
            f"{landed_sha}); re-open a worktree, copy what you want out, then rm -rf it.\n"
            f"{note}")


def _merge_conflict_recovery_pointer(W: Path, label: str, *, _run_git_cap) -> str:
    """T-11264 — the non-union-conflict abort's recovery pointer, or `""` when there is none to give.

    THE MEASURED GAP. `worktree sync` (T-10821, shipped for X-0681) IS the verb that covers this
    seam — it folds the trailing bookkeeping and re-runs this exact merge in place — and the abort
    that blocks the operator named no recovery path at all, so they reached for raw git. The
    evidence is that this is a DISCOVERY gap, not a capability one: bc-community asserted in X-0933
    that NO verb covers it (a reproduction showed one does), `worktree_synced` has fired three times
    in the whole journal, and kupiclub hand-rolled a raw `git commit` at the same seam on
    2026-08-17 (X-0983). The cheap PREDICTIVE surface already names the verb
    (`_land_prequeue_merge_refusal`); the AUTHORITATIVE in-lock refusal did not.

    CONDITIONAL ON THE CALLER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE DIFFICULTY. `_update_from_main` is SHARED by
    `land` and `worktree sync` — that is why the message interpolates a `label` at all. An
    unconditional pointer would make a `worktree sync` abort advise running `worktree sync`, i.e.
    point at itself. So only the LAND caller gets a pointer; every other caller gets `""`.

    FAIL-CLOSED ON ACCURACY. The pointer must be invocable AS PRINTED at the moment it is shown, so
    the form is read off the aborting worktree's actual branch rather than hardcoded: a
    `task/T-NNNN` branch gets the `--task` form, a `work/<slug>` branch gets the `--work` form. Any
    shape `worktree sync` cannot address (detached HEAD, an unreadable ref, an unrecognised prefix)
    yields NO pointer — silence beats a command that would refuse.

    It promises only what the verb does: the fold plus this same merge, worktree intact. Resolving a
    genuinely conflicting SOURCE file afterwards is merge-recovery semantics and is deliberately NOT
    claimed here.
    """
    if label != "land":
        return ""
    try:
        # `_run_git_cap` is documented not to raise, but this is a COSMETIC lookup sitting on a
        # REFUSAL path: it must not be able to become the refusal. A detached HEAD or an unreadable
        # ref already yields an empty branch (nonzero + empty stdout, thanks to `--quiet`) and falls
        # through to "" below; the guard covers everything else — an OSError from the spawn, a
        # substituted callable that raises, anything at all — so the answer degrades to "no pointer"
        # and the merge-non-union-conflict abort reaches the operator exactly as it would have.
        branch = _run_git_cap(["symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "--short", "HEAD"], W).stdout.strip()
        m = re.fullmatch(r"task/(T-\d{4,})", branch)
        if m:
            form = f"yitc-v2 worktree sync --task {m.group(1)}"
        elif branch.startswith("work/") and branch[len("work/"):]:
            form = f"yitc-v2 worktree sync --work {branch[len('work/'):]}"
        else:
            return ""
    except Exception:                          # noqa: BLE001 — a pointer that raises must not abort
        return ""
    return (f"\nRecovery: `{form}` — the covering verb for this seam. It folds the trailing "
            f"bookkeeping and re-runs this same merge in place (worktree intact, main untouched), "
            f"so no raw git is needed to get here again.")


def _apply_land_merge_resolution(W: Path, unmerged, resolved, *, _run_git_cap,
                                 _dedup_events, write_text_atomic):
    """WRITE OUT the `(resolved, unresolved)` verdict `_classify_land_merge_conflicts` produced, stage
    every path, and COMMIT the merge. Returns the commit's `CompletedProcess` — the CALLER decides what
    a nonzero return means, because its two callers mean different things by it.

    EXTRACTED VERBATIM from `_update_from_main` by T-11648 for the reason T-10821 extracted
    `_update_from_main` itself: a SECOND copy of this applier is exactly the parallel path CHARTER
    §Principle 5 forbids. The three arms, their order, the per-path `git add` and the final
    `commit --no-edit` are unchanged value-for-value; only the home moved.

    THE SECOND CALLER IS WHY THIS EXISTS. `_land_merge_batch_into_candidate` merges a batch PEER into
    the candidate, and until this card it was a bare `git merge` with no resolver of any kind — while
    the predictor that ADMITTED that peer (`_land_merge_probe`) judges compatibility by running
    `_classify_land_merge_conflicts` and composing its RESOLVED tree. So a pair whose only conflicts
    were auto-resolvable was called compatible by the predictor and then failed the merge, over and
    over, deterministically (31 `candidate-merge-failed` removals across 21 branches in 11 days,
    measured 2026-08-29). That is the same two-oracles-for-one-question defect T-11216 closed on the
    eviction path, in the opposite direction: there the probe was STRICTER than the land path, here it
    was LOOSER than the merge. This function is what lets both seams resolve identically.

    NOTE THE ASYMMETRY THIS DOES NOT REMOVE: the caller must have ALREADY established that
    `unresolved` is empty. Nothing here re-checks admission — it applies a verdict, it does not form
    one — so passing it a set the classifier did not fully resolve would write a partial resolution
    and commit a conflicted tree. Both callers gate on `unmerged and not unresolved` before entering.
    """
    for f in unmerged:
        kind, payload = resolved[f]
        if kind == "text":
            # keyed union of both sides' per-key values, each carried verbatim from the
            # side that authored it (no value is recomputed or reformatted).
            (W / f).write_text(payload, encoding="utf-8")
        elif kind == "journal":
            # union the two conflict stages (`:2:`=ours, `:3:`=theirs), keep-first dedup —
            # never drop journal rows (mirror of `_fold_main_journal_into_branch`).
            ours = _run_git_cap(["show", f":2:{f}"], W)
            theirs = _run_git_cap(["show", f":3:{f}"], W)
            lines = []
            if ours.returncode == 0:
                lines += ours.stdout.splitlines()
            if theirs.returncode == 0:
                lines += theirs.stdout.splitlines()
            merged = _dedup_events(lines)
            fp = W / f
            fp.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            # T-10755: ATOMIC, same class as the fold's rewrite — a foldable journal is
            # never truncate-rewritten in place (see `_fold_main_journal_into_branch`).
            write_text_atomic(fp, "".join(ln + "\n" for ln in merged))
        else:
            # derived (step 3 rebuilds it) + take-latest `.yitc/` YAMLs → landing branch wins.
            _run_git_cap(["checkout", "--ours", "--", f], W)
        _run_git_cap(["add", "--", f], W)
    return _run_git_cap(["commit", "--no-edit"], W)


def _update_from_main(W: Path, *, label: str, _run_git_cap, _die, _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS,
                      _dedup_events, write_text_atomic,
                      _anchor_signature_of_text=None, _resolved_out=None, source=None) -> None:
    """THE update-from-main step — `git merge --no-edit main` into the writing worktree `W`, with the
    fail-closed per-path auto-resolve classification, else a clean `merge --abort`. Extracted VERBATIM
    from `_land_integrate` step 2 by T-10821 (X-0681) so `worktree sync` can run the SAME step
    standalone instead of keeping a second copy — a second update-from-main would be exactly the
    parallel path CHARTER §Principle 5 forbids. ONE impl, two callers (anti-cx F1: extend one helper).

    `label` names the CALLER (`land` / `worktree sync`). It prefixes both abort MESSAGES, and since
    T-11264 it also selects the non-union-conflict abort's recovery pointer — see
    `_merge_conflict_recovery_pointer`, which must never point the sync caller back at itself. The
    `abort_class` values are UNCHANGED either way, so `land`'s repeated-abort backstop keeps
    streaking on the same stable keys.
    Everything else is byte-identical to the pre-extraction body: same merge, same admission via
    `_classify_land_merge_conflicts` (T-10845, per PATH not per set), same fail-closed fall-through on
    anything no resolver can PROVE is mechanical.

    `_resolved_out` (T-11907) is a REPORTING sink and nothing else: an optional dict the caller
    passes to learn WHICH paths this step auto-resolved, so `worktree sync` can name them on its own
    `worktree_synced` row (AC3 — a resolution the journal never saw is indistinguishable from a hand
    merge, which is the defect the card exists to remove). It is written only on the branch that
    actually applied a resolution; no gate, no ordering and no exit code depends on it, and `land`
    passes nothing, so its behaviour is untouched.

    This function NEVER touches `main` — it merges main INTO `W`'s branch. Everything AROUND the step
    (land's optimistic-concurrency retry, its `merged_base` capture, the graph rebuild, the ff-only
    advance of main) stays with the caller, which is exactly why `worktree sync` can reuse it without
    inheriting any part of the integration.
    """
    # Update from main — union auto-resolves events.jsonl; a conflict confined to committed
    # DERIVED artifacts (graph/index.json — both sides regenerate it) is spurious and gets
    # auto-resolved here, since step 3 rebuilds it deterministically anyway (T-0077, mirrors
    # the events.jsonl union driver). Any OTHER conflict — or a non-conflict merge failure —
    # aborts cleanly (worktree intact, main untouched).
    # `source` (T-12150, audit-post r11 MED) — the ref this step merges. DEFAULT `None` means the
    # SYMBOLIC `main`, which is byte-identical to every pre-existing caller. A caller that has
    # already CAPTURED main's tip passes that IMMUTABLE SHA so the ref it recorded and the ref git
    # merges are provably the same object: between a queued pre-merge's `rev-parse main` and its
    # merge, main can advance A -> B, and merging the symbolic name then processes B while the
    # watermark records A — which invalidates the very equality land's step-2 consumption is proven
    # by. The abort MESSAGES keep saying "from main" (they describe the relationship, not the
    # spelling of the ref), so the abort text and `abort_class` are unchanged on every path.
    mg = _run_git_cap(["merge", "--no-edit", source or "main"], W)
    if mg.returncode != 0:
        # T-12014: decoded + splitlines — each path is matched against the derived-artifact set AND
        # handed back to git as a `checkout --ours` pathspec, so an escaped form failed both.
        unmerged = [q for q in (textutil.git_unquote_path(ln).strip()
                                for ln in _run_git_cap(["diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U"], W).stdout.splitlines()) if q]
        # Auto-resolvable conflicts: committed DERIVED artifacts (both sides regenerate them) +
        # a CONSUMER's tracked `.yitc/` per-session state (T-9204, F-008 residual). T-0871 already
        # folds the SAME `_is_yitc_session_state` class on the UNCOMMITTED-main side; a live
        # interactive consumer that REWROTE the awareness-ledger on BOTH the branch and main makes
        # it a COMMITTED-both-sides content conflict here — which fell through to the non-union abort
        # (kupiclub T-0001, 2× → repeated-abort backstop). Resolve it the SAME way: the foldable
        # journal (`.yitc/events.jsonl`) UNIONS both conflict stages (no dropped rows, AC2), the
        # take-latest YAMLs (awareness-ledger / journal-sync) + the derived artifacts take `--ours`
        # (the landing branch wins; derived is rebuilt deterministically at step 3). INERT for the
        # engine (gitignores `.yitc/`). FAIL-CLOSED: any conflict OUTSIDE this explicit set still aborts.
        # T-10548 (X-0417): the THIRD auto-resolvable class — a spec whose conflict is confined to
        # the machine-managed `implements_signature:` block. Two workers re-stamping DIFFERENT
        # anchors of one spec write ADJACENT sorted lines, which git conflicts even though the
        # sides assert nothing contradictory (boomrocket T-0191, 2× in one hour). T-10845 adds the
        # FOURTH: a shared ROW-APPEND ledger whose conflict is confined to label-keyed rows (19 of
        # 45 measured aborts). Each resolver returns its keyed-3-way merge, or None for anything it
        # cannot PROVE is its class — incl. a same-key divergent value — which falls through to the
        # abort below. Same fail-closed shape throughout: explicit classes, never a widening to source.
        #
        # T-10845 — admission is PER PATH, not per set. The prior test (`set(unmerged) <= admitted`)
        # let ONE unresolvable path void the classification of every other, and the abort then named
        # the whole set as "the conflicts". Now a mixed set of admitted classes resolves and lands,
        # and a genuinely-blocked land names only its true blocker.
        # T-11291: the same-anchor re-derivation reads the MERGED working tree this merge just
        # produced. Absent an injected signer the seam stays dark and the same-anchor case aborts
        # exactly as it did before this card (fail-closed by construction).
        _sig = (_merged_worktree_anchor_signer(
                    W, unmerged, _anchor_signature_of_text=_anchor_signature_of_text)
                if _anchor_signature_of_text is not None else None)
        # T-11907 — WHY a path was refused, collected beside WHICH paths were. Report-only: it
        # feeds the abort TEXT below and no verdict, so an empty sink is a message without a
        # per-path clause, never a different decision.
        reasons: dict = {}
        resolved, unresolved = _classify_land_merge_conflicts(
            unmerged, W, _run_git_cap=_run_git_cap,
            _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS=_DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS,
            _merged_anchor_signature=_sig, _reasons_out=reasons)
        if unmerged and not unresolved:
            cc = _apply_land_merge_resolution(
                W, unmerged, resolved, _run_git_cap=_run_git_cap,
                _dedup_events=_dedup_events, write_text_atomic=write_text_atomic)
            if cc.returncode != 0:
                _run_git_cap(["merge", "--abort"], W)
                _die(f"{label}: could not auto-resolve derived/session-state merge conflict "
                     f"({', '.join(unmerged)}) (worktree intact, main untouched):\n{cc.stderr or cc.stdout}",
                     abort_class="derived-conflict-unresolved")
            elif _resolved_out is not None:
                # T-11907 — REPORTING ONLY, and only on the branch where the resolution actually
                # applied cleanly. `resolved` maps path -> (kind, payload); the sink carries the
                # paths and their kinds, never the merged payloads (a journal row records WHAT was
                # resolved and by which class, not the file contents).
                _resolved_out["paths"] = sorted(resolved)
                _resolved_out["kinds"] = {k: resolved[k][0] for k in sorted(resolved)}
        else:
            # T-10845: name ONLY the paths nothing could resolve. Composing the WHOLE message in
            # this one branch (rather than printing a note above a generic tail) keeps exactly one
            # diagnosis reaching the operator — the resolvable paths are reported as resolvable,
            # never as blockers, so the reader is not told two contradictory things at once.
            # T-11907: a path that produced a diagnosis carries it INLINE, so the refusal names the
            # HUNK it could not resolve — for a keyed record, the KEY — and not merely the file.
            # A path with no diagnosis prints exactly as it always did.
            conflicts = "\n".join(
                (f"{pth}\n    could not resolve: {reasons[pth]}" if pth in reasons else pth)
                for pth in unresolved) if unresolved else (mg.stderr or mg.stdout).strip()
            extra = ""
            if unresolved and resolved:
                extra = ("\n(auto-resolvable, NOT blocking — they no longer void the others: "
                         + ", ".join(sorted(resolved)) + ")")
            # T-11264: the recovery pointer is appended LAST, after the T-10845 diagnosis is fully
            # composed — it names the covering verb, it does not participate in the diagnosis. It is
            # conditional on the caller (a sync abort must not advise `worktree sync`) and empty
            # whenever no accurate form can be rendered. Computed AFTER `merge --abort` on purpose:
            # a cosmetic lookup on a refusal path must not be able to sit between the conflict and
            # the cleanup. `merge --abort` restores HEAD to the same branch, so the answer is
            # identical either side of it — only the failure ordering differs.
            _run_git_cap(["merge", "--abort"], W)
            pointer = _merge_conflict_recovery_pointer(W, label, _run_git_cap=_run_git_cap)
            _die(f"{label}: merge from main has non-union conflicts (worktree intact, main untouched):\n{conflicts}{extra}{pointer}",
                 abort_class="merge-non-union-conflict")


